/* @catalog — generated by `npm run catalog:generate`; do not edit by hand.
 * Internal
 *   Types: Client
 *   Functions: slot, mbid, tags, project, runOf, json, searchUrl, searchAnswer, releaseAnswer, clientOf, drive,
 *              sent, aliasLookups, one, fixture, creditedAnswer, aliasAnswer, recordedReleases,
 *              recordingAnswer, songSearch, albumSearch, collectionAnswer, itunesPaths, reviewRunOf
 *   Constants: guard, importId, actor, start, recorded, blueArchiveTag
 * Tests
 *   - fallback queries are sent together only when the searches before them found nothing; a skipped folder fallback spends no folder allowance
 *   - a comparison is partial when a search returned fewer hits than it reported, the bound left a hit out or stopped a planned query, a search was refused, or the record predates the field
 *   - a release that cannot match never makes a comparison partial: one the bound left out that reported another track count, one that no longer exists, and one whose details failed after reporting another count
 *   - details of releases whose reported track count can match are fetched first
 *   - a 503/429 is busy and retried after its Retry-After within 2–10 s, later after 2 and 5 minutes, then unavailable; a gate busy or unavailable failure keeps 30 s
 *   - Blue Archive tags spelled Sound Track: both exact searches are empty, so the normalized fallbacks are sent together and find the CD and digital editions as a tie
 *   - Hotel California at the 100-release page: nine-track editions are admitted and fetched first, the bound stops the bare search, and the digital edition ties with the European CD
 *   - Pandora Call: a complete recording search still preselects the single group, singles admitted first
 *   - the bound reached with only fallbacks left keeps the comparison complete; a recording search with more hits than it returned is partial, while release-ID and ISRC lookups are complete
 *   - a request maps every failure for the stored step: a refused gate is busy, a 503/429 rate-limited, other source failures and errors unavailable, refused input skipped and a stop a shutdown
 *   - a throttled release lookup is retried within seconds as busy, and details that stay unavailable leave their candidates failed in a completed, partial run
 *   - after the details, a run looks up the aliases of its credited artists and first label once each, never a placeholder, and ranks with them
 *   - alias lookups are bounded to 16 per run, a source failure fails every remaining target and ranks at once without an attempt, and a record without aliases completes
 *   - alias targets follow the ranking with the preselection first, each release's credits then its first label, each ID once, never a placeholder or an unfetched release, at most 16
 *   - an import run fetches a release whose tracks carry credits, an invalid one included, stores them compactly, never looks up a track-only artist and ranks as without them
 *   - Pandora Call romanized: MusicBrainz finds nothing, iTunes gives the Japanese title, and the bridge finds the singles in MusicBrainz
 *   - a barcode is looked up in iTunes when MusicBrainz finds nothing for it, and the collection it names is searched again in MusicBrainz
 *   - a MusicBrainz matching group never starts the iTunes stage, and a run planned from identifiers iTunes cannot search has none
 *   - MusicBrainz exhausted while searching: iTunes is still asked, nothing is bridged, and its result becomes an iTunes-only candidate preselected as not checked
 *   - MusicBrainz exhausted during the bridge leaves the results not yet bridged unavailable, and they become not-checked candidates
 *   - iTunes throttled through its automatic retries: 2–10 s, then 2 and 5 minutes, then the stage is unavailable and the run completes on MusicBrainz's answer
 *   - refused iTunes, bridge and collection requests are skipped: the next request is sent, a refused bridge offers its result unchecked and a refused collection offers none
 *   - per attempt the iTunes stage sends at most five iTunes searches or UPC lookups, four bridge searches and four collection lookups, sharing the 25-candidate bound
 *   - busy mode lasts only until the first failed attempt of either source, and a record from an earlier release without the iTunes fields completes
 *   - the aliases after the iTunes stage are those of the bridged releases' credits, within the bound of 16 the whole run shares, and an iTunes-only candidate adds none
 *   - review alias targets add each fetched release's audio-track artists after its credit and first label, once each, never a placeholder or an iTunes credit, within 16; import targets unchanged
 *   - a review run admits and fetches the fewest extra tracks first and records both completeness flags: a left-out larger release blocks only the only-matching-group preselection, one that could have exactly as many members blocks the exact-count one too
 *   - a review run with a matching group never starts iTunes, while an empty Album's run, whose candidates pair trivially but never match, starts the iTunes stage
 *   - a review run's iTunes answer keeps collections with at least as many tracks as members or an unknown count, fewest extra first, within four; an import keeps its exact counts
 *   - a review run whose iTunes stage leaves out only a collection with more tracks than members preselects its exact-count group; one of unknown count leaves a tie
 *   - a review run's bridge search admits its releases fewest extra first and records both completeness flags
 *   - a typed Artist is never evidence: the preselection's artist flag compares the release with the tags or primary names
 *   - a record a later release stored with Track-title recording searches after its title searches sends them as ordinary searches while searching, never to iTunes, and once past the search phase completes without them
 * @end-catalog */

/**
 * One lookup run driven step by step without a database (lookupRunStep.ts): fallback queries sent together only when the searches
 * before them found nothing, when a comparison is partial (and when a left-out or incomplete release cannot match, so it is not),
 * plausible releases admitted and fetched first, how each request failure is mapped, throttled retries within seconds (searches
 * and release lookups) and the 30-second schedule's grant bound, and the live smoke's albums replayed from recorded and derived
 * fixtures (Blue Archive spelled "Sound Track", Hotel California at the 100-release page, Pandora Call). Requests go through the
 * real client to a fixture transport; nothing reaches the network. Names, files and accounts are synthetic or come from recorded
 * public fixtures.
 */
